Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final  Type  Surgical Pathology  Specimen Type  Breast core needle biopsy  Procedure Date  REMOVED_DATE  Ordering Provider  JAMES E PEPPRIELL M D  PH D   CASE  BS 09  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME   Resident  Qinghong Yang  M D   Ph D   Pathologist  Hongbo Yu  M D   Ph D   PATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS   PART A 1  RIGHT BREAST  UPPER OUTER  CORE BIOPSIES   ATYPICAL LYMPHOID PROLIFERATION WITH MARKED EOSINOPHILIA  see note   Sections show fibroadipose tissue with nodular lymphoid proliferation composed  mainly of small lymphocytes  There is a polymorphous inflammatory infiltrate  with abundant eosinophils  neutrophils  histiocytes and scattered plasma cells   There are scattered large mononuclear cells  likely immunoblasts  Areas of  necrosis  eosinophil abscesses and giant cell reactions are seen   Immunohistochemical studies performed at BWH show that the lymphocytes are  comprised of a mixture of  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   B cells predominantly forming nodules and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    T cells mainly between the nodules  The scattered large mononuclear cells are   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    LCA    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    BSAP    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   BOB 1   OCT 2  and Fascin   likely  immunoblasts  In situ analysis of EBV encoded RNA  EBER  is negative  Desmin  highlights vascular wall but is negative around or within the circular necrotic  areas  Elastic stain highlights vascular wall but is difficult to interpret  whether it is positive around or within the circular necrotic areas  Steiner   Gram  AFB  MSS stains are negative for organisms   NOTE   The findings are of an atypical lymphoid proliferation with marked  eosinophilia  This could represent an intramammary lymph node  Etiology for  marked eosinophilia is not clear  but considerations include response to  infection  parasite exposure or unusual injection of antigenetic protein   Diagnostic features of lymphoproliferative disorder or carcinoma are not seen   Clinical correlation is advised   This case was reviewed by Dr  G  Pinkus  Hematopathology service  and Dr  G   Murphy  Dermatopathology Service  and they concur    Clinical  89 year old female with a palpable mass at right breast upper outer  quadrant  no history of breast cancer   CLINICAL DATA   HISTORY  89 female with new palpable mass 9 00 Right  looks like abnormal  lymph node on mammo with irregular margin  hypoechoic  DDx   metastatic lymph  node  primary breast carcinoma   BREAST IMAGING FINDING   1  11 x 11 x 6mm 9 30 N 11 12cm  Palpable and irregular mass   LOCATION   1  upper outer  TYPE OF CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY   1  14G  TISSUE SUBMITTED   1  14G 5 samples  3 35 p m  in formalin   Brown cassette  GROSS DESCRIPTION   The specimen is received in formalin   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit  number  and  Right breast brown cassette   and consists of multiple tan white  to tan yellow lobulated fibroadipose breast tissue cores  1 0 x REMOVED_DATE x 0 1cm in  aggregate   The breast tissue cores are received in a brown cassette and are  submitted in toto   Micro  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Multi frags  ESS   SEK mmm  CASE NUMBER   REMOVED_CASE_ID   By his her signature below  the senior physician certifies that he she  personally conducted a microscopic examination   gross only  exam if so stated   of the described specimen s  and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis es   related thereto   Final Diagnosis by Hongbo Yu M D   Ph D   Electronically signed on Friday  REMOVED_MONTH 24   REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 01 53 09PM   report_end  , No.
